Z||000033||Cliff Pennock||05-15-99||02:11 PM||||Good! Like you said, <b>now</b> we're getting somewhere! The bad news is, that it still probably is something with IE5... For some reason, your IE does not know how to handle the jpegs it gets from the other server. The only difference is probably the content-type header a server sends before sending an image. Whenever a server sends something to the client, it is preceded with something like: "Content-type: text/html" or "Content-type: x-image/jpeg" etc. Your IE5 receives a header it doesn't recognise and will therefor not show it. I'll check to see what the exact header is it sends, and get back to you. You should be able to change some settings so it does understand it.
